---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitsLifeSizeConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitsLifeSizeConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="clothing-size-converter">
  <UnitsLifeSizeConverter client:load mode="clothing" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>A dress size is not a measurement</h2>
    <p>
      No major market has a legal definition of clothing sizes. A US 8, a UK 12 and a German 38 are
      conventions, and for decades they have grown larger (vanity sizing). A garment labelled US 8
      today is several centimetres bigger than a US 8 from the 1970s.
    </p>
    <p>
      For this reason, this clothing size converter goes from a body measurement to a label, not from
      label to label. You get one approximation, not two.
    </p>

    <h2>Measuring properly</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Bust or chest: around the fullest point, with the tape level all the way round and arms down.</li>
      <li>Waist: around the narrowest part of the torso, usually just above the navel. Do not hold your breath.</li>
      <li>Hip: around the widest point of the seat, usually 20 cm below the waist.</li>
      <li>Neck: for shirts, around the base of the neck with one finger under the tape.</li>
      <li>Inside leg: from the crotch to the top of the shoe, in the shoes you will wear.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Measure over light clothing or none, with the tape snug but not compressing. A tight tape gives
      you a size too small.
    </p>

    <h2>The European numbering families</h2>
    <p>
      "EU size" is not one system. Women&apos;s clothing has at least three common systems, offset from
      each other by fixed amounts:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">US</th><th scope="col">UK</th><th scope="col">Germany / EU</th><th scope="col">France</th><th scope="col">Italy</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>4</td><td>8</td><td>34</td><td>36</td><td>40</td></tr>
        <tr><td>6</td><td>10</td><td>36</td><td>38</td><td>42</td></tr>
        <tr><td>8</td><td>12</td><td>38</td><td>40</td><td>44</td></tr>
        <tr><td>10</td><td>14</td><td>40</td><td>42</td><td>46</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      France is two above Germany; Italy is six above. A label of "40" can be a UK 8, a UK 12 or a UK 14,
      depending on the country that printed it. Look for a country code on the tag.
    </p>

    <h2>Men&apos;s sizing is more honest</h2>
    <p>
      Men&apos;s tailoring usually shows the real chest measurement in inches, and shirts show the
      collar measurement, so the conversion is almost mechanical: a 40 in chest is a 100 cm chest, which
      is an EU 50. Letter sizes (S, M, L) are a coarser layer on the same measurements. When a brand
      gives both, trust the number.
    </p>

    <h2>Jeans, where labels are fiction</h2>
    <p>
      Denim waist labels are the least reliable numbers in clothing. Independent tests found jeans
      labelled W32 that measured from 79 to 91 cm at the waistband, and the same brand varied between
      cuts. The label is a marketing decision, not a measurement.
    </p>
    <p>
      The reliable method: lay a pair that fits flat, measure the waistband from side to side, double
      it, and shop with that number. The jeans tab converts between the inch label, centimetres and the
      European and women&apos;s numbering, but no chart can correct a brand that mislabels.
    </p>

    <h2>Use the brand&apos;s chart when there is one</h2>
    <p>
      These tables are industry mid-points. Some brands publish garment measurements, for example a
      "flat measurement" chart with the dimensions of the garment and not of the intended body. Such a
      chart is much more precise, and you should always use it first.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
